The converse half is proved.  If \(p\in\mathcal M_{\mathcal I}^{\circ}\), then every generator of \(J_{\mathcal I}\) vanishes at \(p\).  Strict positivity of the cells and positivity of every recorded marginal denominator give \(s(p)
eq0\), so \(p\) is a real point of the saturated variety.  Hence \(F_{D,y}\in K_{\mathcal I}\) implies \(F_{D,y}(p)=0\).  Since \(B_{D,y}(p)B_{D_0,y}(p)>0\), cross-multiplication gives \(T_{D,y}(p)=T_{D_0,y}(p)\).  Conversely, \(F_{D,y}
otin K_{\mathcal I}\) yields only a real point of the saturated equality variety where \(F_{D,y}
eq0\).  The frozen assumptions do not imply that this point has positive cells, satisfies every bit-zero inequality, or has rational coordinates.  The certified point \(p^\circ\) proves that one rational positive exact-oracle component is nonempty, but does not put a rational point on the component detected by \(F_{D,y}\).
